WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.639 --> 00:00:11.990 align:start position:0%
 
well there have been  several incidents  two really serious ones that stick in  the mind  uh first one was in 1982  when the volcano mount gullum gong  went off and there was a british airways  flight  that  was flying at 36 000 feet and all the  engines stopped  and the pilots  had a nightmare over time but they they  got down to about 11 000 feet  and they they managed to restart the  engines  the second case was  about seven years later over mount red  out in alaska  in that case they were at about 40 000  feet all four engines stopped  and eventually they managed to restart  and uh  make a safe landing  before the um gulengong  incident we all thought it was oh you  just get a bit of a bit of wear on these  blades because it's sandy and evasive  and you might block up some holes in the  turbines  that of course is a long-term  deterioration it doesn't stop the  engines  the thing that stops the engines and  there was a a very  long and careful investigation  after both the events but there was a  huge report in the  u.s geological survey which is  interesting that it's the geologists  getting involved as well as the  engineers  which shows that the  contents of these volcanic clouds  contain a lot of  particles which are rather like glass  and what happens is that  these particles they enter the engine  and they get to the combustion chamber  here  which is like a furnace  well over a thousand degrees centigrade  the particles get into the combustor and  then this  liquid glass which is a sort of thick  syrupy liquid sprays over the turbines  and it um  partially blocks the air passages so  that um the right amount of air can't  get to can't get through the engine  anymore that's the fan  which sucks air in that's the compressor  which compresses it then it  the fuel is squirted into the combustor  where which of course gets very hot and  then the hot gases expand out to the  turbines  and the turbines are there to to drive  the compressor and it's the  the nozzle guide vanes here and the  turbine blades that follow that are  covered in the glass i mean the damage  to the compressors is quite serious but  it isn't that which shut the engine down  yes i went to a talk by the  the pilots of the the gun gun  event  who told a very moving and  dramatic story  but  the first thing they saw was  sort of sparks and a glow of light in  front of the windscreen and the  passengers could see flashes of light  from the wing leading edges  and streaks of flame  or in orange light coming out of the  both the back and the front of the  engines  which was all these particles glowing  the effect was to  sort of sandblast the windscreen so they  couldn't see through it anymore when  they came into land they had great  difficulty and one of the pilots was  standing up and talking to the other  pilot and they were like there was just  a tiny  lit clear bit of glass right at the edge  of the windscreen  and  they came in you know basically on  instruments but for the last bit you do  need to see where you're going  some people might think they're being  overly cautious by grounding all these  planes what do you think about that the  gun gun passengers formed an association  and have formed the club and having been  on an airplane for i think it was  something more than 11 minutes without  power and writing notes to your nearest  and dearest and thinking you end had  come  now at least two plane loads of  passengers have had that experience  and i don't think anybody else would  like the experience  the aero engine manufacturers do all  they can to  to cope with the effects of dust but  there's no way you can cope with coating  your turbine in glass it's just  all you can do is  just avoid it and and all the  effort since has been  keep out of the clouds

